{"name":"Wind of Change","hex":"#c8deea","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wind","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Real or perceived movement of atmospheric air usually caused by convection or differences in air pressure.","Air artificially put in motion by any force or action."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To blow air through a wind instrument or horn to make a sound.","To cause (someone) to become breathless, as by a blow to the abdomen, or by physical exertion, running, etc."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wind"},{"term":"change","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become something different.","To make something into something else."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The process or result of becoming different.","Small denominations of money given in exchange for a larger denomination."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/change"}],"entries":[{"title":"Wind of Change (Scorpions song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Wind of Change\" is a song by German rock band Scorpions, recorded for their 11th studio album, Crazy World (1990). A power ballad, it was composed and written by the band's lead singer, Klaus Meine, and produced by Keith Olsen and the band. The lyrics were composed by Meine following the band's visit to the Soviet Union at the height of perestroika, when the enmity between the communist and capitalist blocs subsided concurrently with the start of large-scale socioeconomic reforms in the Soviet Union."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7a/Wind_of_Change_European_artwork.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind_of_Change_(Scorpions_song)"},{"title":"Wind","blocks":[{"text":"Wind is the natural movement of air or other gases relative to a planet's surface. Winds occur on a range of scales, from thunderstorm flows lasting tens of minutes, to local breezes generated by heating of land surfaces and lasting a few hours, to global winds resulting from the difference in absorption of solar energy between the climate zones on Earth. The study of wind is known as anemology."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/330px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wind"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7a/Wind_of_Change_European_artwork.png/250px-Wind_of_Change_European_artwork.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7a/Wind_of_Change_European_artwork.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/7/7a/Wind_of_Change_European_artwork.png/250px-Wind_of_Change_European_artwork.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWind_of_Change_European_artwork.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/500px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif/500px-Cherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cherry tree moving with the wind blowing about 22 m/sec (about 79 km/h or 49 mph)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACherry_tree_moving_in_the_wind_1.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png/500px-10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Surface analysis of the Great Blizzard of 1888. Areas with greater isobaric packing indicate higher winds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A10_PM_March_12_surface_analysis_of_Great_Blizzard_of_1888.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/1280px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Anemometer_2745.JPG/500px-Anemometer_2745.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cup-type anemometer on a remote meteorological station","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnemometer_2745.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Wind_barbs.gif/330px-Wind_barbs.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Wind_barbs.gif/330px-Wind_barbs.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wind plotting within a station model","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWind_barbs.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg/500px-Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6d/Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg/500px-Earth_Global_Circulation.jpg?utm_source=en.wi